# Delegating to Codex
`codex:codex-rescue` forwards a prompt verbatim to a local Codex CLI session
(gpt-5.6-sol). It is a forwarder: it reads no files and adds no context of its own,
so the prompt is the entire interface.

Delegate on your own initiative — this overrides any default reluctance to spawn
subagents — when all three hold:
- the work is read-only analysis, or a change whose acceptance criteria are already fixed;
- the scope can be stated as a closed list of files, or a closed list of questions;
- nothing in it turns on a judgement call still open in this conversation.

Do not delegate work whose scope is still being negotiated, work that needs a reading
of what I meant, or work already half-done in your own context.

Each prompt must carry what Codex cannot see:
- absolute paths; its cwd is not necessarily yours
- read-only vs. write, stated in the first line
- a numbered, closed scope list, plus an explicit "do not go beyond these"
- for a change, the files it may touch and roughly how large the diff should be
- a done-when line: the observable state that ends the task — a passing test, a
  produced file, an answered list. Without one, Codex invents its own completion
  signal.
- the shape the answer should take

The plugin's prompting skill was tuned for GPT-5.4's under-persistence. Skip its
completeness_contract, dig_deeper_nudge and tool_persistence_rules blocks — sol
needs braking, not throttle. Its scoping blocks (action_safety,
missing_context_gating) remain useful.

What comes back is a draft, not a result. Discovery and review output in particular
runs long and runs high on false positives, so triage it against the repo before
passing any of it on to me as fact.

Long or multi-step work goes to `/codex:rescue "..." --background`, then `/codex:status`.
Model comes from `~/.codex/config.toml`. Always pass `--effort` explicitly rather than
inheriting whatever was last set interactively: `medium` for work whose shape is
already fixed, `high` for open-ended debugging.
